  <!-- =========== MOTION IN A PLANE (2D) =========== -->
  <section id="plane" class="screen">
    <div class="scr-head">
      <button class="back-btn" onclick="go('home')">‹ Back</button>
      <div>
        <div class="scr-title">Motion in a Plane</div>
        <div class="scr-sub">Promote r, v, a to vectors, differentiate componentwise, and lean on one principle: perpendicular motions are independent.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rev-chips" id="plTabs">
      <button class="rev-chip active" data-pane="plVec">1 · Vector Recap</button>
      <button class="rev-chip" data-pane="plKin">2 · Vectors in Motion</button>
      <button class="rev-chip" data-pane="plInd">3 · Independence</button>
      <button class="rev-chip" data-pane="plEqn">4 · Equations Per Axis</button>
      <button class="rev-chip" data-pane="plQuiz">5 · Rapid Fire</button>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Pane 1 · Vector Recap ── -->
    <div class="grav-pane active" id="plVec">
      <div class="pl-notes">
        <div class="pl-notes-body">
          <h4>1 · Vector Recap</h4>
          <p>A vector A at angle θ to the x-axis resolves into components:</p>
          <div class="pl-nf">A<sub>x</sub> = A cos θ &nbsp;&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp; A<sub>y</sub> = A sin θ</div>
          <p>To recombine the components back into the full vector:</p>
          <div class="pl-nf">|A| = √(A<sub>x</sub>² + A<sub>y</sub>²) &nbsp;&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp; θ = tan⁻¹(A<sub>y</sub> / A<sub>x</sub>)</div>
          <div class="pl-nbox">
            <b>Rule:</b> Always resolve a vector into perpendicular (x, y) components before doing kinematics.
            We solve along the axes, never along a slanted direction directly.
          </div>
          <div class="pl-neg pl-egtap" role="button" tabindex="0">
            <span class="pl-eglab">Example</span>
            Resolve 10 m/s directed at 37° above the horizontal (sin 37° ≈ 0.6, cos 37° ≈ 0.8). Find v<sub>x</sub> and v<sub>y</sub>.
            <span class="pl-eghint">Tap to reveal the answer</span>
            <div class="pl-egans">
              v<sub>x</sub> = 10 cos 37° = 10 × 0.8 = 8 m/s &nbsp;·&nbsp; v<sub>y</sub> = 10 sin 37° = 10 × 0.6 = 6 m/s
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="kin-controls">
        <div class="kin-ctrl">
          <label>Magnitude A <span class="kin-val" id="plVecMagVal">25</span></label>
          <input type="range" id="plVecMag" min="10" max="40" step="1" value="25">
        </div>
        <div class="kin-ctrl">
          <label>Angle θ <span class="kin-val" id="plVecAngVal">37°</span></label>
          <input type="range" id="plVecAng" min="0" max="90" step="1" value="37">
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="kin-canvas" id="plVecCanvas"></div>
      <div class="rev-readouts" style="margin-top:14px;">
        <div class="rev-readout" id="plVecResolve"></div>
        <div class="rev-readout" id="plVecRecombine"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="rev-keybox" style="margin-top:12px;">
        <b>Resolve before you compute.</b> Any vector at angle θ splits into A<sub>x</sub> = A cos θ (green) and A<sub>y</sub> = A sin θ (red).
        Recombine with Pythagoras for the magnitude and tan⁻¹ for the direction. We always work along the axes, never along the slant.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Pane 2 · Vectors in Motion ── -->
    <div class="grav-pane" id="plKin">
      <div class="pl-notes">
        <div class="pl-notes-body">
          <h4>2 · Vectors in Motion</h4>
          <div class="pl-nf">Position: &nbsp; r = x î + y ĵ</div>
          <div class="pl-nf">Velocity: &nbsp; v = dr/dt = v<sub>x</sub> î + v<sub>y</sub> ĵ</div>
          <div class="pl-nf">Acceleration: &nbsp; a = dv/dt = a<sub>x</sub> î + a<sub>y</sub> ĵ</div>
          <div class="pl-nbox">
            <b>Differentiation in 2D is componentwise.</b> The x-part of v depends only on the x-part of r,
            and the y-part of v only on the y-part of r. They never interact.
          </div>
          <p>The velocity vector always points <b>tangent to the path</b>, in the direction of motion.</p>
          <div class="pl-nf">Speed = |v| = √(v<sub>x</sub>² + v<sub>y</sub>²)</div>
          <div class="pl-neg pl-egtap" role="button" tabindex="0">
            <span class="pl-eglab">Example</span>
            r(t) = 3t î + (4t - t²) ĵ. Find v and a.
            <span class="pl-eghint">Tap to reveal the answer</span>
            <div class="pl-egans">
              v = dr/dt = 3 î + (4 - 2t) ĵ &nbsp;·&nbsp; a = dv/dt = 0 î - 2 ĵ<br>
              a<sub>x</sub> = 0, a<sub>y</sub> = -2: this is exactly what a projectile looks like.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="kin-controls">
        <div class="kin-ctrl">
          <label>A particle runs a 2D path <span class="kin-val">r, v tracked live</span></label>
        </div>
        <div class="kin-btns">
          <button class="check-btn" id="plKinPlay">⏸ Pause</button>
          <button class="btn-reset" id="plKinReset">↺ Reset</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="kin-canvas" id="plKinCanvas"></div>
      <div class="rev-readouts" style="margin-top:14px;">
        <div class="rev-readout" id="plKinR"></div>
        <div class="rev-readout" id="plKinV"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="rev-keybox" style="margin-top:12px;">
        <b>Vectors in motion.</b> The position vector r = x î + y ĵ points from the origin to the particle.
        Velocity v = dr/dt is <b>always tangent to the path</b>, and its components v<sub>x</sub>, v<sub>y</sub> are just its shadows on each axis.
        Differentiation is componentwise - differentiate the x-part for the x-answer, the y-part for the y-answer.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Pane 3 · Independence ── -->
    <div class="grav-pane" id="plInd">
      <div class="pl-notes">
        <div class="pl-notes-body">
          <h4>3 · The Independence Principle</h4>
          <p>Motion along perpendicular axes is <b>independent</b>. The x-motion proceeds as if y did not exist,
          and vice versa. The only thing the two share is the clock: time t is common to both.</p>
          <div class="pl-neg pl-egtap" role="button" tabindex="0">
            <span class="pl-eglab">Thrown horizontally vs dropped straight down</span>
            One ball is thrown horizontally (v<sub>x</sub> ≠ 0) and another dropped straight down (v<sub>x</sub> = 0) from the same height at the same instant. Which lands first?
            <span class="pl-eghint">Tap to reveal the answer</span>
            <div class="pl-egans">
              Both share the same vertical motion, so same g, same start height, same fall time → they land together.
              Horizontal velocity does not delay the fall.
            </div>
          </div>
          <p><b>Solve any 2D problem in three steps:</b></p>
          <div class="pl-nstep"><b>Step 1</b> · Resolve into x and y components.</div>
          <div class="pl-nstep"><b>Step 2</b> · Solve each axis as a separate 1D problem.</div>
          <div class="pl-nstep"><b>Step 3</b> · Recombine the results, linked by the shared time t.</div>
          <div class="pl-nbox">
            <b>TIME IS THE BRIDGE.</b> The x-problem and y-problem are solved separately, but both must use the same t.
            Almost every 2D question is: find t from one axis, use it in the other.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="kin-controls">
        <div class="kin-ctrl">
          <label>Horizontal launch speed <span class="kin-val" id="plIndVxVal">12 m/s</span></label>
          <input type="range" id="plIndVx" min="4" max="24" step="2" value="12">
        </div>
        <div class="kin-btns">
          <button class="check-btn" id="plIndGo">Drop &amp; Throw</button>
          <button class="btn-reset" id="plIndPause">⏸ Pause</button>
          <button class="btn-reset" id="plIndReset">↺ Reset</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="kin-canvas" id="plIndCanvas"></div>
      <div class="rev-keybox" style="margin-top:14px;">
        <b>The marker demo, made a principle.</b> One ball is thrown horizontally, one is dropped, at the same instant.
        The amber connectors prove they are at the <b>same height at every tick</b> - the horizontal velocity never delays the fall.
        The x-motion cannot touch the y-motion. Crank the launch speed up: they still land together.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Pane 4 · Equations Per Axis ── -->
    <div class="grav-pane" id="plEqn">
      <div class="pl-notes">
        <div class="pl-notes-body">
          <h4>4 · Equations of Motion, Per Axis</h4>
          <p>For constant acceleration, the three equations apply to each axis independently:</p>
          <div class="pl-ncols">
            <div class="pl-eqcol">
              <div class="pl-eqhead" style="color:#16a34a">x-axis</div>
              <div class="pl-eqrow">v<sub>x</sub> = u<sub>x</sub> + a<sub>x</sub>t</div>
              <div class="pl-eqrow">x = u<sub>x</sub>t + ½a<sub>x</sub>t²</div>
              <div class="pl-eqrow">v<sub>x</sub>² = u<sub>x</sub>² + 2a<sub>x</sub>x</div>
            </div>
            <div class="pl-eqcol">
              <div class="pl-eqhead" style="color:#e11d48">y-axis</div>
              <div class="pl-eqrow">v<sub>y</sub> = u<sub>y</sub> + a<sub>y</sub>t</div>
              <div class="pl-eqrow">y = u<sub>y</sub>t + ½a<sub>y</sub>t²</div>
              <div class="pl-eqrow">v<sub>y</sub>² = u<sub>y</sub>² + 2a<sub>y</sub>y</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pl-neg pl-egtap" role="button" tabindex="0">
            <span class="pl-eglab">Worked example</span>
            A particle starts at the origin with u = (3î + 4ĵ) m/s and constant a = (0î - 2ĵ) m/s². Find v and r at t = 2 s.
            <span class="pl-eghint">Tap to reveal the answer</span>
            <div class="pl-egans">
              <b>x-axis:</b> v<sub>x</sub> = 3 + 0 = 3 m/s &nbsp;·&nbsp; x = 3(2) = 6 m<br>
              <b>y-axis:</b> v<sub>y</sub> = 4 - 2(2) = 0 &nbsp;·&nbsp; y = 4(2) - ½(2)(2²) = 8 - 4 = 4 m<br>
              <b>Answer:</b> v = 3î + 0ĵ (purely horizontal) &nbsp;·&nbsp; r = 6î + 4ĵ → position (6, 4) m.<br>
              At t = 2 s, v<sub>y</sub> = 0: that is the top of a trajectory. A projectile solved without naming it.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="kin-controls">
        <div class="kin-ctrl">
          <label>u<sub>x</sub> (start v<sub>x</sub>) <span class="kin-val" id="plEqUxVal">3 m/s</span></label>
          <input type="range" id="plEqUx" min="0" max="8" step="1" value="3">
        </div>
        <div class="kin-ctrl">
          <label>u<sub>y</sub> (start v<sub>y</sub>) <span class="kin-val" id="plEqUyVal">4 m/s</span></label>
          <input type="range" id="plEqUy" min="0" max="14" step="1" value="4">
        </div>
        <div class="kin-ctrl">
          <label>a<sub>y</sub> (only y accelerates) <span class="kin-val" id="plEqAyVal">-2 m/s²</span></label>
          <input type="range" id="plEqAy" min="-4" max="-1" step="1" value="-2">
        </div>
        <div class="kin-ctrl" style="flex:2;">
          <label>Scrub time t <span class="kin-val" id="plEqTVal">0.00 s</span></label>
          <input type="range" id="plEqT" min="0" max="4" step="0.05" value="0">
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="kin-canvas" id="plEqCanvas"></div>
      <div class="pl-eqtable" id="plEqTable"></div>
      <div class="rev-keybox" id="plEqNote" style="margin-top:12px;"></div>
      <div class="rev-keybox" style="margin-top:12px;">
        The same three equations of motion run <b>independently on each axis</b>. Here a<sub>x</sub> = 0 (constant v<sub>x</sub>) and only y accelerates -
        which is exactly a projectile. Scrub to where v<sub>y</sub> = 0 and you have found the top of the trajectory, without ever calling it one.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Pane 5 · Rapid Fire ── -->
    <div class="grav-pane" id="plQuiz">
      <div class="rev-keybox">
        Eight quick questions on vectors, independence and the resolve-solve-recombine strategy. All guaranteed-marks exam readings.
      </div>
      <div class="rev-card">
        <div class="rf-tag" id="plqTag"></div>
